We propose a novel method for identifying maximum cliques in a given graph by harnessing the capabilities of Gaussian Boson Sampling. This approach is grounded in the observation that the output of an n-photon interferometric experiment can be represented as a graph state's overlap with a set of non-overlapping states. Building on this concept, we develop a scalable method for identifying maximum cliques in graphs comprising up to 15 nodes. Furthermore, we demonstrate the potential of this method to resolve graph isomorphism and graph non-isomorphism problems. Notably, we also show that Gaussian Boson Sampling can efficiently solve a specific type of graph isomorphism problem that is notoriously challenging with conventional computing resources. The presented method offers a promising pathway to resolving computationally intractable problems using Gaussian Boson Sampling. The accompanying code for our analysis is available online at the specified repository. The presented method offers a novel pathway to resolving computationally intractable problems using Gaussian Boson Sampling. The accompanying code for our analysis is available online at the specified repository. The presented method offers a novel pathway to resolving computationally intractable problems using Gaussian Boson Sampling. The accompanying code for our analysis is available online at the specified repository. 
